{
  "summary": "Ensure libbitcoinkernel validation entry points do not mutate or reuse cached validation flags on caller-provided block objects. When invoking `btck_block_check` or `btck_chainstate_manager_process_block`, validation now runs on a shallow copy of the block containing fresh validation state flags while sharing transaction pointers.",
  "problem": "In the kernel C API, `btck_Block` wraps a shared `CBlock`. Once validation marks `block.fChecked` under one set of consensus parameters (e.g., regtest or a permissive signet challenge), any subsequent check on the same shared block instance bypasses full validation under different parameters, allowing invalid blocks to pass validation or be accepted by a chainstate manager. Additionally, modifying `fChecked` inside `btck_block_check` mutates caller state without holding `cs_main`.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description notes: 'a regtest block fails btck_block_check with mainnet params and ALL flags. After it has been checked once with regtest params, the same call with mainnet params returns 1.'",
    "PR description demonstrates a bug where a signet block valid under an OP_TRUE challenge is connected by a second chainstate manager configured with an OP_RETURN challenge, leaving the second chain unable to read its own tip back via ReadBlock.",
    "PR description reports two new test cases in `test_kernel.cpp` reproducing the issues, which fail on master with four failures and pass with this change."
  ],
